Objective 1:  Most Frequently Used Chemicals

i i. General Description:
This objective aims to identify the most frequently occurring chemicals across cosmetic and personal care product formulations. Frequent usage of specific ingredients can indicate industry trends, consumer demand, and formulation practices. It also helps spotlight ingredients that may require closer scrutiny due to their high exposure potential. By isolating the top chemicals based on occurrence counts, this analysis helps in prioritizing ingredient monitoring, research on health impacts, and potential reformulation pathways for safer alternatives.
Understanding which chemicals dominate product compositions also provides a foundation for further analysis, such as linking chemical prevalence with product categories (e.g., makeup vs. skincare), evaluating associated health risk levels, or identifying recurring ingredients in products labeled as “natural” or “hypoallergenic.”
ii. Specific Requirements:
To execute this analysis, the following analytical and visualization parameters were established:
•	Extraction of unique chemical names from the dataset and calculation of their frequency of occurrence.
•	Filtering and sorting to retrieve the top 10 most common chemicals.
•	Construction of a vertical bar chart using Python's Seaborn and Matplotlib libraries.
•	Application of a color gradient from soft blue to deep violet for aesthetic differentiation and improved visual storytelling.
•	Clear labeling of chemical names on the x-axis and corresponding frequency counts on the y-axis.
•	Interactive annotations (in dynamic versions) or static frequency labels above each bar to enhance readability.
•	Structuring the chart in descending order to prioritize interpretation of most-used chemicals first.
iii. Analysis Results:
Upon analyzing the cleaned dataset, the following chemicals emerged as the top contenders based on frequency of appearance across products:
1.	Titanium Dioxide – Widely used for its UV filtering properties and as a white pigment.
2.	Mica – Frequently added for shimmer and texture in makeup products.
3.	Iron Oxides – Commonly used in foundations and lipsticks for natural pigmentation.
4.	Fragrance (Parfum) – Found in a vast majority of scented personal care products.
5.	Talc – Used for its smooth texture and moisture-absorbing properties.
6.	Petrolatum – A common emollient found in lotions and skin balms.
7.	Zinc Oxide – Recognized for UV protection and soothing properties.
8.	Phenoxyethanol – A popular preservative in skincare formulations.
9.	Dimethicone – A silicone-based compound used for smooth application and moisture retention.
10.	Caprylyl Glycol – Often employed as a skin-conditioning agent and preservative booster.
These top-ranking ingredients reveal a pattern of repeated use of specific functional components such as UV filters, preservatives, colorants, and fragrancing agents, suggesting their irreplaceable role in modern formulations.
iv. Visualization:
To effectively communicate these insights, a vertical bar chart titled “Top 10 Most Frequently Used Chemicals in Products” was developed using Python. The visualization employs a gradient color scheme ranging from light blue (lower frequency) to deep violet (highest frequency) for immediate visual differentiation.
Each bar is clearly labeled with the chemical name and the corresponding frequency count is annotated above the bar. The chart follows a descending order, placing the most used chemicals on the left for rapid interpretation. This visualization aids stakeholders—including regulators, formulators, and consumers—in identifying chemical usage patterns and focusing attention on high-exposure substances.

Objective 2: Chemical Usage by Cosmetic Category
i. General Description:
This objective investigates the distribution of chemical ingredients across various cosmetic product categories. By quantifying chemical usage within groups such as Skincare, Haircare, Makeup, and Fragrance, this analysis helps uncover patterns in ingredient selection and formulation behavior specific to each category. Understanding which product categories contain the most diverse or dense chemical compositions is critical for both regulatory focus and consumer awareness.
Such insights can inform ingredient safety assessments, influence clean beauty initiatives, and support brands in choosing alternative ingredients that align with both performance and safety. This objective also supports industry professionals in tracking category-specific innovation and formulation trends.
ii. Specific Requirements:
The following methodology and visualization techniques were employed to accomplish this analysis:
•	Categorization of products into distinct cosmetic segments such as Skincare, Haircare, Fragrance, Makeup, Personal Hygiene, etc.
•	Aggregation of chemical ingredient counts within each category.
•	Development of a bar graph using Seaborn and Matplotlib in Python to represent the frequency of chemical use by category.
•	Usage of contrasting color schemes for each bar to clearly differentiate product categories.
•	Inclusion of x-axis labels for product categories and y-axis values representing total chemical usage.
•	Optional enhancement with interactive tooltips or static annotations above bars to show exact counts.
•	Ordering categories by descending chemical frequency for better clarity and comparative assessment.
iii. Analysis Results:
The visualization revealed the following trends in chemical usage across cosmetic categories:
•	Skincare products demonstrated the highest concentration of ingredients, with numerous formulations incorporating a wide range of actives, emulsifiers, stabilizers, and preservatives.
•	Makeup products, particularly foundations and lipsticks, also showed substantial chemical density due to their color additives, texturizers, and long-wear enhancers.
•	Haircare formulations ranked third, reflecting the extensive use of surfactants, conditioning agents, and fragrances.
•	Fragrance products utilized fewer unique chemicals, although many included high-impact compounds known for allergenicity or persistence.
•	Personal hygiene and baby care categories had comparatively simpler compositions, often containing mild and limited ingredient profiles.
These results reflect a direct correlation between product functionality and formulation complexity, where multifunctional products tend to rely on a broader chemical palette.
iv. Visualization:
The final bar graph, titled “Chemical Ingredient Usage by Cosmetic Category”, presents a clear comparative view of chemical distribution across product types. Each bar represents a specific category, colored using a distinctive palette for immediate visual separation.
The bars are arranged in descending order of chemical frequency, making it easy to interpret which categories are most ingredient-heavy. The chart includes labeled axes, count annotations, and maintains a clean layout for presentation in reports, dashboards, or public awareness campaigns.
This visualization plays a key role in category-level formulation analysis and helps in identifying where regulatory, sustainability, or reformulation efforts could be most impactful.

Objective 3: Map Brand vs. Chemical Risk
i. General Description:
This objective aims to map the relationship between cosmetic brands and the risk levels of the chemicals they use in their product formulations. By analyzing how frequently high-risk or moderate-risk ingredients are associated with specific brands, the analysis provides a transparency-focused perspective on formulation practices across the industry. This enables consumers, regulators, and formulators to assess brand commitment toward ingredient safety and clean product development.
The mapping of chemical risks by brand is crucial in identifying brands that consistently rely on low-risk, safe ingredients, as well as highlighting those that may frequently use substances flagged for health or environmental concerns. This can fuel informed decision-making, clean beauty advocacy, and risk-based formulation redesign.
ii. Specific Requirements:
To achieve this objective, the following steps and visualization components were implemented:
•	Classification of chemicals based on risk levels (e.g., Low, Moderate, and High Risk).
•	Aggregation of chemical occurrences per brand, broken down by associated risk category.
•	Visualization using a stacked bar chart or grouped bar chart in Python (Matplotlib/Seaborn), allowing risk level breakdown per brand.
•	Distinct color coding: e.g., green for low-risk, orange for moderate-risk, and red for high-risk ingredients.
•	Brand names on the x-axis and number of chemical occurrences on the y-axis.
•	Tooltips or annotations (optional) for detailed insights per bar segment.
•	Selection of top 8–10 most prominent brands to keep the visualization readable and focused.
iii. Analysis Results:
The brand-risk mapping revealed notable trends:
•	Some leading cosmetic brands exhibited a higher percentage of low-risk ingredients, showcasing efforts towards clean and safe formulation strategies. These brands tended to have stronger transparency and alignment with sustainability goals.
•	A few brands, however, showed considerable usage of moderate to high-risk chemicals, which may include parabens, formaldehyde releasers, or persistent fragrances flagged by health watchdogs.
•	Brands in mass-market product lines often exhibited higher risk profiles compared to niche or premium brands, which tended to invest in safer alternatives.
•	Notably, a couple of emerging brands demonstrated complete avoidance of high-risk ingredients, reflecting a new wave of conscious formulation backed by consumer trust and data-led decision-making.
This mapping not only illustrates risk variations but also brings brand accountability and ingredient literacy to the forefront of product evaluation.
iv. Visualization:
The bar chart titled “Brand vs. Chemical Risk Level” provides a comparative view of chemical safety across cosmetic brands. Each bar is segmented by risk level color blocks, offering a quick understanding of ingredient risk distribution per brand. The visual prioritizes readability and contrast, aiding in stakeholder engagement, regulatory briefing, or educational outreach.
This visual insight supports risk-based product auditing, enables brands to benchmark their formulation safety, and promotes a shift toward cleaner, consumer-conscious cosmetic innovation.

Objective 4: Diversity of Chemical Use
i. General Description:
This objective focuses on evaluating the diversity of chemical usage across various cosmetic products. By analyzing how the number and types of chemicals vary over different cosmetic categories or timeframes, the study uncovers patterns that reflect the complexity and formulation strategies adopted in the industry. Understanding chemical diversity is critical for assessing formulation richness, innovation trends, and ingredient repetition or uniqueness across product lines.
The exploration of diversity provides insights into whether certain product types rely heavily on a limited set of chemicals or embrace a broader spectrum of ingredients, which may reflect either advanced formulations or increased exposure risk due to complexity.
ii. Specific Requirements:
To visualize and quantify the diversity of chemical use, the following analysis components were implemented:
•	Count of unique chemicals used per cosmetic category (or over time, if applicable).
•	Grouping of data based on cosmetic type, such as shampoos, lipsticks, moisturizers, etc.
•	Measurement of chemical variety (diversity index) per group.
•	Visualization using a line graph to capture the trend of chemical diversity across categories.
•	X-axis represents cosmetic categories; Y-axis represents the count of distinct chemicals used.
•	Points along the line marked for better traceability and comparison.
•	Optional trendline or smoothing applied to highlight progressive changes or peak complexity zones.
iii. Analysis Results:
The diversity analysis revealed several interesting patterns:
•	Hair care and skincare products showed the highest diversity of chemicals, likely due to the multifunctional nature of these products (e.g., cleansing, moisturizing, conditioning).
•	Color cosmetics (e.g., lipstick, eyeliner) demonstrated moderate diversity, often using targeted pigment blends and preservatives.
•	Sunscreens and serums, while more focused, featured specialized chemical sets, resulting in lower overall diversity but high active ingredient concentration.
•	A few categories showed chemical overlap, suggesting formulation reuse or standardized ingredient sets in related product families.
•	The line graph clearly depicted peaks in chemical diversity, hinting at formulation complexity and consumer expectations for product performance in certain segments.
These insights can inform both formulation innovation strategies and regulatory focus areas by identifying product categories with potentially high chemical exposure diversity.
iv. Visualization:
A line graph titled “Chemical Diversity Across Cosmetic Categories” was developed using Python libraries such as Matplotlib and Seaborn. The graph presents a smooth visual of unique chemical counts per category, with each data point labeled for clarity. The line design helps capture subtle increases or decreases in diversity, enhancing visibility of complexity trends across product types.
This visualization supports ingredient optimization, formulation benchmarking, and category-specific risk management, laying a foundation for safer and more transparent cosmetic development.

Objective 5: Chemical Count vs. Product Longevity
i. General Description:
This objective investigates the relationship between the number of chemicals used in a product and its longevity (i.e., shelf life or period of use). By comparing chemical complexity with product lifespan, the analysis aims to determine whether a higher count of ingredients correlates with longer-lasting formulations or indicates over-formulation. This exploration provides valuable insight into formulation efficiency, preservative impact, and potential safety considerations in cosmetics.
Understanding this correlation is essential for manufacturers seeking to balance product performance with safety, cost, and regulatory compliance. It also provides researchers and consumers with a clearer picture of how chemical composition might influence product durability.
ii. Specific Requirements:
To carry out this analysis effectively, the following components were considered:
•	Calculate the total number of chemicals used in each cosmetic product entry.
•	Extract or compute product longevity values (e.g., duration in months or years).
•	Use a scatter plot to visualize the relationship between chemical count (X-axis) and product longevity (Y-axis).
•	Apply color coding or transparency to distinguish overlapping data points and improve readability.
•	Optionally fit a trendline (regression line) to observe the overall pattern or correlation.
•	Annotate or highlight notable clusters or outliers.
iii. Analysis Results:
The scatter plot analysis revealed the following key observations:
•	Products with a moderate number of chemicals (10–25) often exhibited the longest longevity, suggesting a balance between functionality and preservation.
•	Items with very high chemical counts (30+) did not necessarily guarantee longer shelf life, indicating diminishing returns or formulation inefficiencies.
•	Products with fewer ingredients (less than 10) typically showed shorter longevity, possibly due to minimal preservative content or natural formulations.
•	A weak to moderate positive correlation was observed, showing that while higher chemical counts can contribute to longer product life, it's not a strict rule.
•	Several outliers were identified—products with few chemicals but long longevity, potentially due to potent preservatives or innovative packaging.
These findings can support R&D and quality control teams in optimizing formulations for longevity while minimizing unnecessary chemical use.
iv. Visualization:
A scatter plot titled “Chemical Count vs. Product Longevity” was generated using Python with Seaborn and Matplotlib. Each point on the chart represents an individual cosmetic product, with the X-axis showing the number of chemicals and the Y-axis representing the product's longevity.
The use of scatter distribution reveals clusters, trends, and outliers effectively. A regression line was optionally added to visualize the general correlation. This chart provides a clear, data-driven view of formulation strategy and its impact on the usable life of cosmetic products.
This objective supports formulation science, regulatory assessments, and consumer safety evaluations, all aimed at ensuring long-lasting and well-balanced products

Objective 6: Removal of Outliers Using the Capping Method
This objective focuses on data preprocessing and outlier management by applying the capping method to numerical features within the dataset. Outliers—extreme values that deviate significantly from the rest of the data—can distort statistical analysis and impact model accuracy. Capping, also known as winsorization, involves limiting outlier values by setting them to a specific percentile threshold, effectively reducing noise and enhancing data stability.
This approach ensures that extreme data points do not unduly influence the visualization or interpretation of chemical usage patterns, product longevity, or category-wise statistics.
.

ii. Specific Requirements:
o To effectively identify and cap outliers, the following steps were undertaken:
•	Select relevant numeric columns such as chemical count, product longevity, or ingredient concentration.
•	Calculate interquartile range (IQR) to determine lower and upper bounds for acceptable data (typically 1.5 × IQR below Q1 and above Q3).
•	Apply capping:
o	Replace values below the lower bound with the 5th percentile value.
o	Replace values above the upper bound with the 95th percentile value.
•	Validate the impact by comparing pre- and post-capping distributions using descriptive statistics and box plots.


iii. Analysis Results:
The capping process yielded several improvements in data consistency:
•	Outliers in chemical count and product longevity were significantly reduced, especially those influenced by rare or mislabeled products.
•	The spread of values became more compact, leading to more reliable visualizations and trend analysis.
•	Central tendencies (mean and median) remained stable, while standard deviation was reduced, indicating lower variance.
•	Subsequent analyses (e.g., scatter plots and bar charts) produced more balanced insights and fewer skewed interpretations.

iv. Visualization:
A combination of box plots and histograms was used to illustrate the effectiveness of capping. Before-and-after comparisons clearly showed the compression of extreme values and normalization of distributions.
This outlier handling step was essential for enhancing the quality of insights drawn from the dataset, particularly when dealing with large-scale consumer product data. The use of the capping method contributed to a cleaner, more trustworthy dataset, setting the foundation for robust visual and statistical exploration across all objectives.
